    "pageLabel": "Page 42",
    "photoUrl": "/practitioner_guide/photos/PG_Page_042_ShowingUp_IMG_5630.JPG",
    "lineCount": 34,
    "rawText": "Invitation→Learning Lab It's often said that“Good is the enemy of great.\" Shadow Here is a crucial revision: Avoidance is the enemy of the great. It is even the Avoidance enemy of the good. Avoid an ce keeps usfrom facingwhat'sreal. It stalls rowth, dulls ad apt ability, N undermines well-being, and blocks the conversations that bring teams, relationships, and cultures alive. We avoid what's un comfortable e motion, conflict, uncertainty —and trade truth for brief relief. The leader who brushes past their own doubt doesn't lead with more confidence. They lead with less information. The couple who avoids the conversation they need to have doesn't have a more peaceful relationship. They have the same relationship, with one more thing they can’t talk about. And the team that never names the tension doesn't have less tension. It has tension plus the cost of pretending otherwise. Strategy doesn’t create the future. People do. Goals don't create the future. People do. How we meet our inner world especially discomfort, shapes how we love live parent, and lead. Three shadow forms of avoidance quietly undermine what people and organizations care about most: Brooding: circling the story until we are trapped inside it. ·Bottling: pushing feelings down until they push back. Forced Positivity: papering over emotion and nuance with hollow cheer. Avoidance is not only silence It can sound professional, supportive, even kind That's why it is sneaky. It can feel protective. We're trying to understand. We're just getting on with it. Yet, it's costly. Possibility begins when we show up to what is hard. Show Up Step Out Walk Your Why Move On 42 @2026SUSANDAVID·Emotional Agility@·VERSION2.0",
