    "lineCount": 34,
    "rawText": "Earth·inside the story Remember what your“hooked” moment felt like when you were fully in it. Example:“I'm terrible at this. I never know what to say\" From here... Iam Iam thinking Iam about to Moon·observing the story Imagine your“hooked”moment is Earth, and you're now looking at it from the Moon. Example:“I notice I go quiet whenever a conversation gets tense.” From this distance... Inotice Ifeel I notice I am thinking From the outside, I can see Jupiter·seeing the story in context Widen the space even further. Imagine you're now on Jupiter, looking back at Earth. Your hooked”moment looks like a tiny marble in a vast sky. Example: “This shows up whenever I feel judged. In this context, I notice it looks smallr than it felt.\" From this distance... In this context, Inoticeit looks like In thiscontext, Inoticewhat's also true is Onemove runs underneath this exercise: noticing. On Earth, the moment is writing us. We're still hooked. On the Moon, we're able to notice and observe. On Jupiter, we can notice the fuller context. The words shift with the view: I am →I notice →in this context, I notice. By changing language, you create psychological space. To notice is tomeeta thought, lingratryrwhatititouhtlingstrytis of the context, not all of it; real, but never the thing that defines the whole. For example, when you say “I am” as in“I am sad”or“I am angry,” it makes you sound as if you are the emotion. Whereas you are you, and the emotion isa data source. I can hold the story without being authored by it. That's one way language makes space. Here are some others. Stepping Out 55",
    "htmlContent": "<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Earth·inside the story Remember what your“hooked” moment felt like when you were fully in it. Example:“I'm terrible at this. I never know what to say&quot; From here...</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Iam</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Iam thinking</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Iam about to</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Moon·observing the story Imagine your“hooked”moment is Earth, and you're now looking at it from the Moon. Example:“I notice I go quiet whenever a conversation gets tense.” From this distance...</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Inotice Ifeel</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">I notice I am thinking</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">From the outside, I can see</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Jupiter·seeing the story in context Widen the space even further. Imagine you're now on Jupiter, looking back at Earth. Your hooked”moment looks like a tiny marble in a vast sky. Example:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"article-quote\">“This shows up whenever I feel judged. In this context, I notice it looks smallr</blockquote>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">than it felt.&quot; From this distance...</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">In this context, Inoticeit looks like</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">In thiscontext, Inoticewhat's also true is Onemove runs underneath this exercise: noticing. On Earth, the moment is writing us. We're still hooked. On the Moon, we're able to notice and observe. On Jupiter, we can notice the fuller context. The words shift with the view: I am →I notice →in this context, I notice. By changing language, you create psychological space. To notice is tomeeta thought, lingratryrwhatititouhtlingstrytis of the context, not all of it; real, but never the thing that defines the whole. For example, when you say “I am” as in“I am sad”or“I am angry,” it makes you sound as if you are the emotion. Whereas you are you, and the emotion isa data source. I can hold the story without being authored by it. That's one way language makes space. Here are some others.</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Stepping Out 55</h3>"
