    "rawText": "Bothness is: ·Holding two truth s without lettingone erase theother ·Stayingwith the tension rather thanrushing toresolveit Av is i b il ty practice not a solution Bothness \"Two sides to everything\":bothness doesn't apply to harm Relativism: a fact anda counter claim don't getequal status A call for com pro rise: youcanhold both truths and still or de human i za tion. We hold safety anddignityfirst isnot: Forced balance: one truthmay mattermore in a context; choose a firm course both ne ss just keeps the other visible Pocket Practice Welcome In 60 secondsor less Dissent One person can hold two truths at once. So can a conversation, when we keep both alive instead of collapsing to one. Welcoming dissent is how. After sharing an idea or during a discussion: Ask openly:“Who can offer a dissenting perspective？” Thank the first voice that speaks up. ·Avoid:“Who agrees?\" It pulls the room toward consensus before alternatives surface. ·Avoid:“Who disagrees?”It labels the person rather than the idea, which reduces psychological safety and learning. Show Up Step Out Walk Your Why Move On 68 @2026SUSANDAVID·Emotional Agility@·VERSION 2.0",
    "htmlContent": "<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Bothness is: ·Holding two truth s without lettingone erase theother ·Stayingwith the tension rather thanrushing toresolveit Av is i b il ty practice not a solution Bothness &quot;Two sides to everything&quot;:bothness doesn't apply to harm Relativism: a fact anda counter claim don't getequal status A call for com pro rise: youcanhold both truths and still or de human i za tion. We hold safety anddignityfirst isnot: Forced balance: one truthmay mattermore in a context; choose a firm course both ne ss just keeps the other visible</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Pocket Practice</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Welcome In 60 secondsor less Dissent One person can hold two truths at once. So can a conversation, when we keep both alive instead of collapsing to one. Welcoming dissent is how. After sharing an idea or during a discussion: Ask openly:“Who can offer a dissenting perspective？” Thank the first voice that speaks up. ·Avoid:“Who agrees?&quot; It pulls the room toward consensus before alternatives surface. ·Avoid:“Who disagrees?”It labels the person rather than the idea, which reduces psychological safety and learning.</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Show Up</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Step Out</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Walk Your Why</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Move On</p>"
