    "htmlContent": "<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Tweak Create one tiny, values-based tweak. Your House of Change likely shows where toplace it. Add the new behavior ontoa habit PIGGYBACK Examples: you already have. I'm a bad parent. At bedtime, I'll ask about their day, and listen to the whole</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">NO-BRAINER answer.(Piggyback)</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">aligned choice is the easiest one. Set upyour emvironment so thevalues- I'm such a slowlearner; I shouldn't even try tolearn something new.→ Ill leave the book open on my pillow, and let One Page count.(No-Brainer)</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">PRE COMMITMENT</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Decide in advance: ifthis happens, Nobody respects me or listens to me.→ If I get cut off, I'll say,“Id like to then Iwill.. finish.&quot;(Precommitment) Picture what could get in the way and planfor howyou'll meet it. OB ST AC LE COURSE Around here, nothing ever really changes.→Before I propose this, rll list what gotin the way last time, and plan for the biggest potential obstacle. (Obstacle Course) My tiny tweak(small, doable, and meaningful) is How does this tiny tweakfeel compared to the original statement? What shifts, in you or between people, when the change becomes smaller and more aligned Tiny tweaks bring play f ul ne ss, curiosity, and experiment a tion into the places whereyou feel stuck. Instead of re making your lifeinone dramatic sweep, you begin shaping it one moment, one choice point, one small step at a time.</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Powerful Question</h3>\n<p class=\"article-paragraph\">Whatis the smallest step I could take today toward this change?</p>\n<h3 class=\"article-heading\">Moving On 107</h3>"
